they don't wanna see,

00:03:36.489 --> 00:03:37.813
and I think it's actually very important

00:03:37.813 --> 00:03:39.726
on Instagram specifically for you

00:03:39.726 --> 00:03:41.747
to segment out all three.

00:03:41.747 --> 00:03:43.640
I do think over time
Instagram will figure that out

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:45.787
as it evolves as a product, or maybe not,

00:03:45.787 --> 00:03:47.366
'cause they wanna make that friction

00:03:47.366 --> 00:03:49.305
so people aren't hitting it too hard

00:03:49.305 --> 00:03:50.489
from too many different directions,

00:03:50.489 --> 00:03:52.788
but to answer your question practically,

00:03:52.788 --> 00:03:53.971
you need to create the three accounts,

00:03:53.971 --> 00:03:54.784
and it's gonna be a pain in the butt

00:03:54.784 --> 00:03:57.558
because you gotta sign out, sign back in,

00:03:58.628 --> 00:04:00.240
but is that really that tough?

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:02.203
- [Voiceover] Rafael asks,

00:04:02.203 --> 00:04:04.759
"They say you should
hire slow and fire quick.

00:04:04.759 --> 00:04:06.766
How many chances do you give your staff?"

00:04:07.776 --> 00:04:08.747
- I feel it's funny.

00:04:08.747 --> 00:04:11.695
On this show, multiple times, I talk about

00:04:11.695 --> 00:04:13.568
not being crippled by hiring somebody

00:04:13.568 --> 00:04:15.556
because if they're not good I'll fire.

00:04:15.556 --> 00:04:17.514
The truth is, I have struggled

00:04:17.514 --> 00:04:20.586
for 15 plus year of my career, at least,

00:04:20.586 --> 00:04:23.121
22, 37, I would say for the
first 15 years of my career

00:04:23.121 --> 00:04:25.444
I was not doing a good enough job

00:04:25.444 --> 00:04:27.500
in the firing department, and it still

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.459
is something I struggle with.

00:04:30.579 --> 00:04:31.724
It's just not fun.

00:04:31.724 --> 00:04:33.698
There's nothing worse
than firing somebody.

00:04:33.698 --> 00:04:35.520
There's nothing good about it.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.869
I usually spend an extra 20 to 30 days

00:04:38.869 --> 00:04:41.123
just figuring out the justification,

00:04:41.123 --> 00:04:43.473
of like, "Oh but they were..."

00:04:43.473 --> 00:04:45.771
I'm literally making
up stuff to make myself

00:04:45.771 --> 00:04:47.709
feel better about it.

00:04:47.709 --> 00:04:50.125
So the truth is, the
real answer for me is,

00:04:51.015 --> 00:04:52.690
we're slow to it, even...

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:53.828
It's one of the things I'm trying to get

00:04:53.828 --> 00:04:55.093
this company better at is,

00:04:55.093 --> 00:04:56.938
don't worry, any
VeynerMedia people watching.

00:04:58.308 --> 00:04:59.708
Yeah, I mean, look.

00:04:59.708 --> 00:05:01.044
I've definitely come to
learn that you're doing

00:05:01.044 --> 00:05:02.395
the right thing for them as well

00:05:02.395 --> 00:05:03.823
'cause you're just
dragging out the process,

00:05:03.823 --> 00:05:05.320
and they're not growing, and nothing good

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:07.120
is gonna come of that, but....

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:12.055
The answer, practically,
is I use my intuition.

00:05:12.055 --> 00:05:13.616
I really do.

00:05:13.616 --> 00:05:15.176
You just have to make a gut call sometimes

00:05:15.176 --> 00:05:18.081
on can you give this
person one more chance?

00:05:18.081 --> 00:05:20.713
We are not in the one, two,
or even three strike policy

00:05:20.713 --> 00:05:22.095
here at Vayner.

00:05:22.095 --> 00:05:23.431
We have enormous continuity,

00:05:23.431 --> 00:05:25.589
and some of it has to do with 
the way we fire, in my opinion.

00:05:25.589 --> 00:05:27.959
I think people see us
trying to handle things

00:05:27.959 --> 00:05:31.836
with empathy and grace, and
one thing I'm very proud of

00:05:31.836 --> 00:05:34.110
is when people are let go here,

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.096
people aren't that surprised, right?

00:05:36.096 --> 00:05:38.405
And so, people paying attention...

00:05:38.405 --> 00:05:39.637
But you don't wanna hold on too long

00:05:39.637 --> 00:05:42.558
because then you lose the
trust of all the great people,

00:05:42.558 --> 00:05:45.452
and so I don't think there's a set answer.

00:05:45.452 --> 00:05:47.112
You gotta go on your intuition.

00:05:47.112 --> 00:05:48.437
I think the more interesting answer is

00:05:48.437 --> 00:05:50.690
do you think you have the
EQ and the people skills

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:53.055
and the intuition to do
it, and if you don't,

00:05:53.055 --> 00:05:54.876
who do you think has it?

00:05:54.876 --> 00:05:56.785
And empower them to do it.

00:05:56.785 --> 00:05:58.095
That's the more interesting part.

00:05:58.095 --> 00:06:00.695
And by the way, that could go left field.

00:06:00.695 --> 00:06:03.726
I would tell you that if
I didn't have that skill

00:06:03.726 --> 00:06:05.224
in my early days of Wine Library,

00:06:05.224 --> 00:06:07.126
I would have courted my mother

00:06:07.126 --> 00:06:08.926
to come in and be that person

00:06:08.926 --> 00:06:10.121
because I knew she could do it,

00:06:10.121 --> 00:06:12.502
so even think outside
of your employee base.

00:06:12.502 --> 00:06:14.859
It might be a friend who
is not happy in their job,

00:06:14.859 --> 00:06:16.263
but you know they have
the best people skills

00:06:16.263 --> 00:06:18.539
you've ever seen, and
maybe you bring them in

00:06:18.539 --> 00:06:20.698
for a pseudo-HR doing other things,

00:06:20.698 --> 00:06:22.641
and you want them to handle that.

00:06:22.641 --> 00:06:26.584
The firing process is immensely important

00:06:26.584 --> 00:06:28.325
in every organization,
one that I don't think

00:06:28.325 --> 00:06:30.159
people put enough emphasis on,

00:06:30.159 --> 00:06:31.564
and there's a lot of angles.

00:06:31.564 --> 00:06:32.783
It's not just firing fast.

00:06:32.783 --> 00:06:35.276
It's not just not firing.

00:06:35.276 --> 00:06:36.860
It's how you fire.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:40.184
We've been letting some people go,

00:06:40.184 --> 00:06:42.523
at least recently, throughout
the last three or four months,

00:06:42.523 --> 00:06:45.238
and I usually am not
that close to it anymore

00:06:45.238 --> 00:06:47.526
because I'm very much
trying to scale this,

00:06:47.526 --> 00:06:50.683
but I make sure that I reach
out a week or two later,

00:06:50.683 --> 00:06:52.982
when I find out, to that
person and thank them,

00:06:52.982 --> 00:06:55.003
if they were here for a day.

00:06:55.003 --> 00:06:56.568
All those things matter.

00:06:57.468 --> 00:06:58.686
- [Voiceover] Ashey asks,

00:06:58.686 --> 00:06:59.541
"Do you think brands need to be

00:06:59.541 --> 00:07:01.902
represented across all social platforms,

00:07:01.902 --> 00:07:03.864
or just the most popular ones?"

00:07:04.835 --> 00:07:07.122
- Ashley, good job by
the double enforcement

00:07:07.122 --> 00:07:08.098
because you put up the Instagram,

00:07:08.098 --> 00:07:09.385
but you also hit me up on Twitter

00:07:09.385 --> 00:07:10.558
and said, "Yo, answer my question."

00:07:10.558 --> 00:07:13.554
I like that gusto. Way to get it, girl.

00:07:15.994 --> 00:07:18.708
I do not think that
every brand in business

00:07:18.708 --> 00:07:19.904
should be on every platform.

00:07:19.904 --> 00:07:21.941
I actually don't also
think that they should be

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:23.339
just on the popular ones.

00:07:23.339 --> 00:07:25.458
If you do not know how to communicate,

00:07:25.458 --> 00:07:28.007
or your audience is
not there, for example,

00:07:28.007 --> 00:07:29.238
if you sell mattr-

00:07:29.238 --> 00:07:32.029
(thoughtful groan)

00:07:32.029 --> 00:07:36.088
If you sell adult diapers,
I would argue that

00:07:36.088 --> 00:07:37.886
SnapChat is not gonna kill it for you,

00:07:37.886 --> 00:07:40.405
even though it is
massively popular, right?

00:07:40.405 --> 00:07:45.256
If you sell selfie sticks
that are only made...

00:07:45.256 --> 00:07:46.941
That are pink and only marketed

00:07:46.941 --> 00:07:49.484
to fifteen year old girls, you know,

00:07:49.484 --> 00:07:51.004
Facebook's starting to become a place

00:07:51.004 --> 00:07:52.919
where you could debate
is not really for you,

00:07:52.919 --> 00:07:55.160
and so I think you need
to be in the right place

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:57.006
regardless of its size.

00:07:57.006 --> 00:07:58.642
YikYak's not the biggest,

00:07:58.642 --> 00:08:00.848
but I think a lot of
college campus pizza shops

00:08:00.848 --> 00:08:03.066
should be all in on that, right?

00:08:03.066 --> 00:08:05.005
So, it's not the size of the platform.

00:08:05.005 --> 00:08:06.026
(laughter)
There's something

00:08:06.026 --> 00:08:06.769
funny about that.

00:08:06.769 --> 00:08:09.087
It's not the size of the platform,

00:08:09.087 --> 00:08:11.980
and it's definitely not on everything.

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:14.049
It's what's right for your business,

00:08:14.049 --> 00:08:16.440
based on who you are trying to reach,

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:18.321
and so if you're looking to try to reach

00:08:18.321 --> 00:08:20.688
60 year old, grey haired
business executives,

00:08:20.688 --> 00:08:22.025
you've gotta look at Linked In

00:08:22.025 --> 00:08:24.149
with a much more crucial
eye than on Instagram.

00:08:24.149 --> 00:08:27.183
If you're trying to reach
25 to 45 year old women,

00:08:27.183 --> 00:08:29.442
now you get into the Instagram
and Facebook game for sure,

00:08:29.442 --> 00:08:30.800
and a little bit less Twitter.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:32.227
Definitely Pinterest as well.

00:08:32.227 --> 00:08:35.602
If you're trying to
reach 28 year old dudes,

00:08:35.602 --> 00:08:37.081
Pinterest not as much, right?

00:08:37.081 --> 00:08:39.369
So, you've gotta understand the demo.

00:08:39.369 --> 00:08:41.888
You're gotta reverse engineer
the demo you're selling to

00:08:41.888 --> 00:08:43.623
or you're aspiring to sell to,

00:08:43.623 --> 00:08:45.126
and that's where you need to story tell.

00:08:45.126 --> 00:08:48.527
Around the attention graph of that person,

00:08:48.527 --> 00:08:52.110
predicated on the platform
that most matters.

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:54.308
- [Voiceover] Jonathan asks,

00:08:54.308 --> 00:08:55.875
"I import wines from Italy and sell

00:08:55.875 --> 00:08:57.721
via e-comm and through distributors.

00:08:57.721 --> 00:08:59.660
I'm currently focusing on
obtaining new distribution

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:01.622
and have fallen behind on the e-comm side.

00:09:01.622 --> 00:09:03.482
Question: What percentage of my time

00:09:03.482 --> 00:09:05.777
should be split between
each side of the business,

00:09:05.777 --> 00:09:08.032
and what's a good way to balance the two?"

00:09:10.203 --> 00:09:11.338
You know, Jonathan, it's interesting.

00:09:11.338 --> 00:09:12.894
I'm trying to give a general answer

00:09:12.894 --> 00:09:16.038
to the whole market, but I
know your business so well.

00:09:16.038 --> 00:09:18.629
When you're importing, I don't
know what state you're in,

00:09:18.629 --> 00:09:20.311
but when you're doing e-commerce,

00:09:20.311 --> 00:09:22.054
are you selling to the end consumer?

00:09:22.054 --> 00:09:24.701
Because you can't do that
in New Jersey or New York.

00:09:24.701 --> 00:09:26.182
Are you in a state-

00:09:26.182 --> 00:09:27.625
He's in California, and Kermit Lynch,

00:09:27.625 --> 00:09:29.680
I think, does do that, so I think he can.

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:31.177
I think in California,
I was about to say that,

00:09:31.177 --> 00:09:32.164
thanks, India.

00:09:32.164 --> 00:09:33.569
In California, 'cause
Kermit Lynch does that,

00:09:33.569 --> 00:09:35.706
you're able to actually
sell to the consumer

00:09:35.706 --> 00:09:37.389
and also sell to distributors.

00:09:37.389 --> 00:09:40.442
You know, to me, if you
can sell e-commerce 100%,

00:09:40.442 --> 00:09:41.371
you're no dope.

00:09:41.371 --> 00:09:42.728
You know that you're making 50 cents more

00:09:42.728 --> 00:09:44.587
on every dollar by selling that way

00:09:44.587 --> 00:09:45.607
than selling to distributors,

00:09:45.607 --> 00:09:47.361
but what you know is you're not that big,

00:09:47.361 --> 00:09:49.962
and you're not necessarily
a retailer by trade,

00:09:49.962 --> 00:09:51.726
and it takes a lot of money and skill

00:09:51.726 --> 00:09:53.973
to be able to be a good
retailer to sell enough,

00:09:53.973 --> 00:09:55.766
where the distributors are
giving you the buying power.

00:09:55.766 --> 00:09:57.566
So my strategy for you is-

00:09:57.566 --> 00:09:59.550
Now, I'm giving you as
black and white a strategy

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:01.616
as I've given on this show's history.

00:10:03.166 --> 00:10:06.609
80% to the distributors, 20% of your time,

00:10:06.609 --> 00:10:08.792
energy, and money to the consumers.

00:10:08.792 --> 00:10:09.988
Once you get that established,

